  "summary": "Graph and higher-order formulations now extend omics analysis beyond independent gene-level values, with one direct benchmark against omics-only analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is representing omics data through network, topological, or higher-order structure rather than as independent gene-level values. PathWeigh II applies graph decomposition and Gaussian belief propagation over directed multigraphs to score pathway activity from expression data. The Triadic Perceptron Model and TRIM algorithm mine higher-order triadic interactions from node metadata in acute myeloid gene expression data. PULSAR links gene, cell, and multicellular representations in a multi-scale foundation model tied to donor-level disease classification. HMLMs treat signaling molecules as tokens over graph-structured cardiac fibroblast data within a transformer framework. What is new is treating the expression profile itself as a graph or embedded network object rather than a vector of values. Scope and maturity differ across these items. Only the triadic study is published; the remaining items are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
